## Phase detection
- **Single peak century**: **1400 BCE** — highest mean `pollen_grate_100` (0.001931 ≈ +0.193% per year), also highest positive-sum (0.224) and highest single-year max (0.00465 at CE ≈ −1415).
- **Sustained high-growth phase**: **1300 BCE and 1400 BCE** — the only *contiguous pair* of centuries whose mean pollen_grate_100 exceeds the window mean (−0.000147). 1300 BCE is the only other century with positive mean pollen_grate_100 (0.001108) without a negative spike dominating. The 3-century centered rolling mean of pollen_grate_100 also peaks at the 1300 BCE center (0.000947), reinforcing the 1300–1400 BCE plateau.
- A third isolated century, 1600 BCE (+0.000874), shows a smaller bump but is preceded by the strongly negative 1500 BCE bin (−0.003020) and is not contiguous with 1400 BCE.

## Corroboration
- `g_all_mean` per-century mean is also highest in 1400 BCE (0.002836) with the 2nd-highest in 1300 BCE (0.001424) — independent KDE-growth signal agrees.
- Net Δ of `pollen_inter` over 1400 BCE = +0.985 (2nd highest after 1600 BCE +1.745 — but 1600 BCE starts from a very low 0.70 baseline after the 1500 BCE collapse, so its cumulative Δ is partly rebound rather than growth from a comparable level).
- capital.csv (12-capital Zmean, n=10 in window) shows Zmean rising monotonically from 1300 BCE (0.664) to **1400 BCE (0.940 — highest in the window)**, then dropping in 1500 BCE (0.261). This directionally corroborates the 1400 BCE peak.

## Sensitivity (C2 binning, one-sentence check)
Under C2 (century = ceil(|CE|/100)), the peak label moves to "1500 BCE" — describing the **same physical ~100-y span** (the bin shifts by 1–2 boundary years; under C2 the bin contains CE≈−1500…−1401). The actual rank order and physical event are unchanged.

## Interpolation caveat
All numeric results above use `_inter`/smoothed columns. The raw pollen series has only 48 non-NaN values in the 2000–1100 BCE window (~95% missing) and the raw capital Z columns have 891/901 missing; the answer is therefore built on interpolated and 100-y smoothed signals, not on raw observations.

## Final hypothesis
The highest-growth century between 2000 BCE and 1100 BCE is **1400 BCE**, supported by the largest positive mean `pollen_grate_100` (≈+0.193% per year), the largest single positive spike (≈+0.46% at CE ≈ −1415 BCE), the largest positive cumulative change in `pollen_inter` (+0.99 Z units), and the highest contemporaneous KDE growth-rate mean `g_all_mean` (+0.00284). The strongest *sustained* growth phase is the contiguous pair **1300 BCE – 1400 BCE**, the only consecutive centuries in the window whose mean pollen openness growth rate exceeds the 1900–1100 BCE window mean of −0.0147%/yr; this interval is also the only place where the secondary KDE-growth signal `g_all_mean` is positive in adjacent bins (1300: +0.00142, 1400: +0.00284). The corroborating capital.csv Zmean composite reaches its window-maximum of +0.94 in 1400 BCE (vs. 0.66 in 1300 BCE and 0.26 in 1500 BCE), directionally confirming the 1400 BCE peak rather than overriding it. Under the alternative binning convention C2 (ceil-of-century), the peak label shifts to "1500 BCE" — but both conventions identify the same physical ≈100-year span (CE ≈ −1500 to −1400); the conclusion is robust. All of the above is built on the `_inter`/smoothed columns (raw pollen and capital Z are ~95% missing in the window), so the result reflects a smoothed and interpolated signal rather than direct observations.
